--- A frame's missing cell: a column shows it as the error it is read as,
--- `error: missing` (a frame's table as `missing`), and its .ok says it is
--- missing. A column that can have missing cells — a join's unmatched
--- side, a database column that can be NULL — has fallible cells: a
--- missing one is an error of reason 'missing', which computes and cannot
--- be decided on, and `fill` gives the column a value where it had none,
--- so its cells are plain again. A column no compiler can type (a file
--- loaded as the program runs) has dynamic cells, and a missing one is
--- JSON's null, an error value. (The interpreter read a missing cell as an
--- error, so a collecting loop over one dropped the row; compiled, a
--- comparison with a cell read did not build; then both read a missing
--- cell as its column's placeholder, NaN or 0 or false — a guess.)
use frame
write['parity65.csv', 'a,b,s\n1,,x\n2,3,\n']
df = load['parity65.csv']
log[df.b]
log[df.s]
log[df.b.ok]
log[df.s[1]]
rm['parity65.csv']
a = frame.new[symbol = ['A', 'B', 'C'], gate = [1, 0, 0]]
b = frame.new[symbol = ['C', 'A'], rsi = [75.0, 20.0]]
j = join[a, b, 'symbol', 'left']
x = j.rsi[1]
log[x.ok]
log[x.reason]
log[x gt 70.0]
log[j.rsi[0] + 1.0]
f = fill[j, 'rsi', 0.0]
hot = for i in range[len[f]]
    f.gate[i] eq 1 or f.rsi[i] gt 70.0
log[hot]
log[len[f[hot]]]
log[len[j[j.rsi gt 70.0]]]
